On 1 Jul 2003 06:52:56 -0700, LDiCesare typed:

> I (a Barb) have cleared all levels in the Gnomish Mines, Sokoban and
> up to Medus'a level, but I have a problem: I have no way of walking
> over water. Maybe one of those cursed rings could do the trick, but I
> can't ID them. I am faced with thee following choices:
> Wait for enough monsters to show up that I can level up and do the
> quest, hopefully finding something which will let me levitate/breathe
> water/water walk.
> Put on the cursed rings and pray. Or pray while having them in
> inventory, hoping my god will uncurse them.
> Go swimming.

Someone wrote up a more complete guide to this question a few months back;
you can probably find it on Google Groups if you hunt around.

That said... Unless you can polymorph yourself into a water creature, you
can't "go swimming". The closest you can get is if you have the right sort
of amulet, which will let you walk across the bottom. You will need to
protect your gear from water damage if you do this.

You say you've done Sokoban. Have you already used those two scrolls that
always turn up in the first Sokoban level? It's tedious, but they can be
used to literally bridge the Medusa moat. Personally, they have long been
my preferred method for getting across, because once the path is built it
is permanent. No worrying about something stealing or destroying your
water-walking equipment.

> I am very much afraid of swimming. There are many eels down there, and
> I tend to sink because of my equipment. What do I need in order to be
> able to swim? Should I remove my dwarven mithril coat? Or just get rid
> of enough miscellaneous equipment?

Unless you have found a way to make yourself slippery or don't need air,
don't go anywhere near eels, with or without water-walking powers.
Thrown/fired rocks make wonderful and cheap eel-killers. Get a pile, stick
them in your quiver, and go to it.

> Also, can am I right when I think that I cannot dig downwards in
> Medusa's level?

No, you *can* dig down. Just don't do it right next to the water.


